      "text": "Since the line passes through $(2,2,1)$ and $(5,1,-2),$ we can write its vector equation as:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 5 - 2 \\\\ 1 - 2 \\\\ -2 - 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nWe want to find the coordinate $z$ when $x = 4.$  Substituting $x = 4$ into the vector equation, we have:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ y \\\\ z \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix} + t \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThis gives us the system of equations:\n\\begin{align*}\n4 &= 2 + 3t, \\\\\ny &= 2 - t, \\\\\nz &= 1 - 3t.\n\\end{align*}\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have:\n\\[\n3t = 2 \\implies t = \\frac{2}{3}.\n\\]\nSubstituting $t = \\frac{2}{3}$ into the second and third equations, we find:\n\\begin{align*}\ny &= 2 - \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{4}{3}, \\\\\nz &= 1 - 3 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3} = -1.\n\\end{align*}\nTherefore, the $z$-coordinate of the point is $\\boxed{-1}.$\nThe answer is: -1",
