      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve for the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point on the line passing through the points \\((2,2,1)\\) and \\((5,1,-2)\\) with an \\( x \\)-coordinate of 4, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the direction vector of the line:**\n   The direction vector \\(\\mathbf{d}\\) of the line can be determined by subtracting the coordinates of the first point from the coordinates of the second point:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{d} = (5 - 2, 1 - 2, -2 - 1) = (3, -1, -3)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Write the parametric equations of the line:**\n   The parametric equations of the line can be written using the point \\((2,2,1)\\) and the direction vector \\((3, -1, -3)\\). Let \\( t \\) be the parameter. Then the parametric equations are:\n   \\[\n   x = 2 + 3t\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = 2 - t\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   z = 1 - 3t\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the value of \\( t \\) when \\( x = 4 \\):**\n   We need to find \\( t \\) such that \\( x = 4 \\). Using the parametric equation for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   4 = 2 + 3t\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( t \\):\n   \\[\n   4 - 2 = 3t\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2 = 3t\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   t = \\frac{2}{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Find the corresponding \\( z \\)-coordinate using \\( t = \\frac{2}{3} \\):**\n   Substitute \\( t = \\frac{2}{3} \\) into the parametric equation for \\( z \\):\n   \\[\n   z = 1 - 3 \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)\n   \\]\n   Simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   z = 1 - 2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   z = -1\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the \\( z \\)-coordinate of the point on the line with an \\( x \\)-coordinate of 4 is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
