      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the $z$-coordinate of the point on the line, we first need to determine the parametric equations of the line passing through the points $(2, 2, 1)$ and $(5, 1, -2)$. The parametric equations of a line in 3D space are given by:\n\n\\[ x = x_0 + at, \\]\n\\[ y = y_0 + bt, \\]\n\\[ z = z_0 + ct, \\]\n\nwhere $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is a point on the line and $(a, b, c)$ is the direction vector of the line. The direction vector can be found by subtracting the coordinates of the first point from the coordinates of the second point:\n\n\\[ (5, 1, -2) - (2, 2, 1) = (3, -1, -3). \\]\n\nSo, the direction vector is $(3, -1, -3)$. Using the point $(2, 2, 1)$ as $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$, the parametric equations of the line are:\n\n\\[ x = 2 + 3t, \\]\n\\[ y = 2 - t, \\]\n\\[ z = 1 - 3t. \\]\n\nWe are given that a point on this line has an $x$-coordinate of 4. We substitute $x = 4$ into the $x$-parametric equation:\n\n\\[ 4 = 2 + 3t. \\]\n\nSolving for $t$:\n\n\\[ 4 - 2 = 3t, \\]\n\\[ 2 = 3t, \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{2}{3}. \\]\n\nNow, we substitute $t = \\frac{2}{3}$ into the $z$-parametric equation to find the $z$-coordinate of the point:\n\n\\[ z = 1 - 3\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = 1 - 2 = -1. \\]\n\nTherefore, the $z$-coordinate of the point is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{-1}. \\]",
